# MSEI-025 | Q4: Hit Counter Using Cookies (PHP)

**Definition**
A cookie is a small key-value pair stored in the browser and sent back to the server with every HTTP request. This program uses a cookie to track how many times a user has visited a page.

**Steps**
1. Create counter.php in the web server document root
2. On each visit: check if visit_count cookie exists
3. If yes: increment value. If no: set to 1.
4. Set cookie with updated value and 1-hour expiry
5. Display current count on the page
6. Refresh browser multiple times to observe counter incrementing

**counter.php**
```php
<?php
  if (isset($_COOKIE['visit_count'])) {
    $count = $_COOKIE['visit_count'] + 1;
  } else {
    $count = 1;
  }
  setcookie('visit_count', $count, time() + 3600);
  echo "You have visited $count time(s).";
?>
```

**How Cookies Work**
```
First visit:  Browser --> GET /counter.php --> Server
              Browser <-- Set-Cookie: visit_count=1 <-- Server

Second visit: Browser --> GET /counter.php + Cookie: visit_count=1 --> Server
              Browser <-- Set-Cookie: visit_count=2 <-- Server
```

**Cookie Security Attributes**

| Attribute | Purpose |
|---|---|
| HttpOnly | Blocks JavaScript from reading the cookie. Prevents XSS-based cookie theft. |
| Secure | Cookie sent only over HTTPS. Prevents interception on HTTP. |
| SameSite | Restricts cross-site sending. Prevents CSRF attacks. |
| Expires/Max-Age | Controls cookie lifetime. |

**Viva**
This counter is client-side controlled. A user can edit the cookie value in DevTools and set visit_count=9999. Server trusts the value sent back. Reliable counters store the count server-side in a database and use the cookie only as a session ID.

