# MSEI-023 | Q1: MITM Attack on Shopping Cart

**Definition**
Client-side MITM: attacker intercepts and modifies data inside the browser (via DevTools) before it is sent to the server. The server receives manipulated values and processes them as legitimate because it performs no server-side price validation.

**Steps**
1. Create shopping_cart.html with a calculate() JavaScript function
2. Open page in browser. Click Calculate. Grand Total = Rs 1,01,500.
3. Press F12 to open DevTools. Go to Elements tab.
4. Find: `<input type='text' id='rate1' value='50000'>`
5. Double-click value '50000'. Change to '1'. Press Enter.
6. Click Calculate again.
7. New grand total = (1 x 2) + (500 x 3) = Rs 1,503.
8. Server receives Rs 1,503 if no server-side validation exists.

**shopping_cart.html (key parts)**
```html
<input type="text" id="rate1" value="50000">  <!-- attacker changes this -->
<span id="grand">101500</span>

<script>
function calculate() {
  var t = parseInt(document.getElementById("rate1").value)
        * parseInt(document.getElementById("qty1").value);
  document.getElementById("grand").innerHTML = t + t2;
}
</script>
```

**Attack Flow**
```
Normal:  Browser calculates 50000x2 + 500x3 = 101500 --> sent to server
MITM:    Attacker edits rate1 to 1 in DOM
         Browser calculates 1x2 + 500x3 = 1503 --> sent to server
         Server trusts client value. Processes order at Rs 1503.
```

**Prevention**

| Method | How it works |
|---|---|
| Server-side price validation | Server looks up price from its own DB. Never trusts client-submitted price. |
| Signed price tokens | Server sends a hash of the price. Tampered value invalidates the hash. |
| Session-based cart | Cart stored server-side. Client can change quantity only, not price. |
| Input validation | Server rejects negative prices and prices outside catalogue range. |
| HTTPS | Encrypts channel, but does NOT prevent DOM manipulation inside the browser. |

**Viva**
HTTPS does not prevent this attack. The manipulation happens inside the browser before the data is sent. HTTPS cannot validate what JavaScript calculated. This is why server-side validation is mandatory regardless of HTTPS.

