# MSEI-023 | Q2: Search Engine Operators

**Definition**
Search engine operators are special syntax commands used to refine search results. In cyber security, they are used during the reconnaissance phase to gather publicly available information about a target. This is called Google Dorking or OSINT (Open Source Intelligence).

**Operators Table**

| Operator | Example | Function |
|---|---|---|
| `"exact phrase"` | `"information security policy"` | Returns pages with this exact string only |
| `OR` | `firewall OR IDS` | Returns pages with either term |
| `AND` | `security AND audit` | Returns pages with both terms |
| `- (minus/NOT)` | `python -snake` | Excludes the word after minus |
| `* (wildcard)` | `"* security framework"` | Asterisk matches any word in that position |
| `site:` | `site:ignou.ac.in syllabus` | Searches only within specified domain |
| `filetype:` | `filetype:pdf security policy` | Returns only specified file type |
| `intitle:` | `intitle:"index of" passwords` | Finds pages with this text in the title |
| `inurl:` | `inurl:admin login` | Finds pages with this text in the URL |
| `cache:` | `cache:example.com` | Shows Google's cached version of the page |

**Example Dorks**
```
filetype:sql site:targetcompany.com      -- exposed DB backups
intitle:"index of" "wp-config.php"       -- exposed WordPress config
inurl:/admin/login site:target.com       -- admin login panels
filetype:xls "username" "password"       -- credential spreadsheets
```

**Viva**
Using these techniques on systems you do not have authorisation to test is illegal under IT Act 2000 Section 43 (unauthorised access). This is passive reconnaissance only; you are querying Google's index, not the target directly. Always obtain written permission before conducting reconnaissance on any organisation.

